---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Soundtrack - Composer, Themes & Instruments';
const description =
  'The Grave Seasons soundtrack from the official devlog: composer Gisula, Suvi Savikko, day and night seasonal themes, and 100% live instrumentation.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Soundtrack', url: '/soundtrack/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.blumhouseMedia, SOURCES.steam];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.farmHarvest.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Audio - official devlog</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      The Grave Seasons Soundtrack
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Perfect Garbage's first behind-the-scenes devlog was about the music, and it is one of the most
      concrete things the studio has published about the game. Everything on this page comes from
      that first-party post.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.farmHarvest.src} alt={ASSETS.farmHarvest.alt} width={ASSETS.farmHarvest.width} height={ASSETS.farmHarvest.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.farmHarvest.credit} - the daytime farming loop that the seasonal day themes score.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>Who writes the music</h2>
      <p>
        Two people. <strong>Suvi Savikko</strong> is Grave Seasons' <strong>Art &amp; Audio
        Director</strong>, and <strong>Gisula</strong> is the game's <strong>composer</strong>. The
        devlog frames the pairing as a deliberate clash of backgrounds: "We all come from different
        musical backgrounds. We're all making farming sim music, but we're making music that we like,
        and that's what's making this soundtrack special."
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that Savikko holds <em>both</em> art and audio direction, which is unusual and explains
        why the score and the pixel art pull in the same tonal direction - cozy on the surface, wrong
        underneath.
      </p>

      <h2>The Main Theme came first</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Main Theme</strong> is described as the team's "meatiest and most difficult track
        to date," and it was the <strong>very first piece composed</strong>. Perfect Garbage say it
        embodies the whole journey of the game, pulling on its design philosophy, folklore and story.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gisula's approach to structure is worth quoting because it explains why the soundtrack is not
        wall-to-wall intensity: "I'm a very visual person when I'm composing. Imagine this as a story
        structure, right? You have the climax of it there and you have to have your little ebbs and
        flows. If the song is big the whole time, then you don't really feel the impact."
      </p>

      <h2>Every season has a day version and a night version</h2>
      <p>
        This is the most useful structural detail in the devlog. The <strong>seasonal themes</strong>
        are split in two, and the split is also a split between composers:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Daytime themes</strong> - composed by Gisula.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nighttime themes</strong> - composed by Suvi Savikko, riffing on the daytime
          version's key or chord progression and putting a darker spin on it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Savikko's stated intent for the night versions: "You want to create the impression that
        things are a little less safe now...a little more ambiguous. You're no longer in that sweet
        and safe farming sim territory. You're in uncharted waters."
      </p>
      <p>
        That lines up exactly with what the studio confirmed about the
        <a href="/day-and-night/">day and night cycle</a> - there is no forced bedtime, and the game
        wants you out after dark. The music is built to tell you that you have crossed a line.
      </p>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img src={ASSETS.townNight.src} alt={ASSETS.townNight.alt} width={ASSETS.townNight.width} height={ASSETS.townNight.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.townNight.credit} - the night versions of each seasonal theme score scenes like this one.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>It is mostly live instrumentation</h2>
      <p>
        The devlog is specific about how the tracks were recorded, and the answer is "with actual
        instruments." Gisula's figure: many tracks are
        <strong>"90-95% live, 100% live instrumentation and mostly acoustic, so that we get that
        really organic feel to it."</strong> The stated goal was groundedness - making the world feel
        real rather than sampled.
      </p>
      <p>Confirmed experiments listed in the post:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nylon</strong> and <strong>steel-string</strong> guitars.</li>
        <li><strong>Layered live percussion.</strong></li>
        <li>Blended <strong>electric and acoustic cello</strong> textures.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Two Easter eggs the studio told on themselves</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          At least one seasonal theme <strong>spells out the name of its season in Morse code</strong>,
          layered into the track.
        </li>
        <li>
          Gisula recorded <strong>live bird ambience in Nashville</strong> and layered it in - the
          devlog's joke being that all birds there sing in perfect key.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you like hunting that kind of thing, the Morse-code detail is a genuine listening target at
        launch. The devlog does not say <em>which</em> season it applies to, so treat any specific
        claim about that as unverified.
      </p>

      <h2>What is not confirmed</h2>
      <p>
        The devlog previewed <strong>four tracks</strong> in an accompanying video, but Perfect Garbage
        have <strong>not</strong> published a track list, a total runtime, or any plan for a separate
        soundtrack release on Steam, Bandcamp or streaming services. There is also no statement about
        whether music is diegetic anywhere in Ashenridge, or how combat and murder-mystery beats are
        scored.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-fog">
        Anything you see claiming a full Grave Seasons OST track list before launch is not coming from
        the developers.
      </p>

      <h2>Where this fits</h2>
      <p>
        The music is the clearest expression of the game's whole design bet: a farming sim that keeps
        the comfort and then quietly removes it. For the visual half of the same idea, see the
        <a href="/map-of-ashenridge/">map of Ashenridge</a>; for the systems the night themes are
        scoring, the <a href="/day-and-night/">day and night cycle</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
